# D8 — Pigeonhole principle behind WallBreaker's split.
# Depicts: with at most k edits distributed over k+1 contiguous pieces, at least one piece survives
# uncorrupted (Standard). Transposition/Merge-Split need 2k+1 because one boundary-spanning edit can
# corrupt two adjacent pieces.
direction: down

title: |md
  # D8 · Pigeonhole — k edits over k+1 pieces ⇒ ≥1 clean piece
| { near: top-center }

standard: "Standard, k = 2 → 3 pieces" {
  style.fill: "#EAECEE"; style.stroke: "#34495E"
  p1: "piece 1\n✗ error" { style.fill: "#FADBD8"; style.stroke: "#E74C3C" }
  p2: "piece 2\n✗ error" { style.fill: "#FADBD8"; style.stroke: "#E74C3C" }
  p3: "piece 3\n✓ clean" { style.fill: "#D5F5E3"; style.stroke: "#2ECC71" }
  p1 -> p2 -> p3: { style.stroke: "#AAB7B8" }
}

clean: "piece 3 is an EXACT substring of the matching term\n→ seed the SCDAWG lookup here" {
  style.fill: "#D5F5E3"; style.stroke: "#2ECC71"
}
standard.p3 -> clean: "uncorrupted" { style.stroke: "#2ECC71" }

why2k: |md
  **Why 2k+1 for Transposition / Merge-Split?** A single boundary-spanning transposition
  (e.g. `"ABCDE" → "ACBDX"`) or merge can corrupt *two* adjacent pieces at once, so k edits
  may dirty up to 2k pieces — 2k+1 pieces guarantee one survivor. (Counts are Coq-verified upstream.)
| { near: bottom-center; style.fill: "#FCF3CF" }
